  "text": "Assistant: ### Step 1: Rewrite the equations of the circles in standard form\nThe standard form of a circle's equation is \\((x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2\\), where \\((h, k)\\) is the center and \\(r\\) is the radius.\n\nFor \\(C_1: x^2 + y^2 - 2x - 4y + 1 = 0\\), we complete the square:\n\\[\nx^2 - 2x + y^2 - 4y = -1\n\\]\n\\[\n(x^2 - 2x + 1) + (y^2 - 4y + 4) = -1 + 1 + 4\n\\]\n\\[\n(x - 1)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 4\n\\]\nSo, \\(C_1\\) has center \\((1, 2)\\) and radius \\(2\\).\n\nFor \\(C_2: x^2 + y^2 + 2x - 6y + 2 = 0\\), we complete the square:\n\\[\nx^2 + 2x + y^2 - 6y = -2\n\\]\n\\[\n(x^2 + 2x + 1) + (y^2 - 6y + 9) = -2 + 1 + 9\n\\]\n\\[\n(x + 1)^2 + (y - 3)^2 = 8\n\\]\nSo, \\(C_2\\) has center \\((-1, 3)\\) and radius \\(2\\sqrt{2}\\).\n\n### Step 2: Find the slope of the line passing through the centers of \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\)\nThe slope \\(m\\) of the line passing through \\((1, 2)\\) and \\((-1, 3)\\) is:\n\\[\nm = \\frac{3 - 2}{-1 - 1} = \\frac{1}{-2} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Determine the slope of the tangent line\nWe need the tangent line to be perpendicular to this line passing through the centers. The slope \\(m_t\\) of the tangent line is the negative reciprocal of \\(m\\):\n\\[\nm_t = \\frac{-1}{-\\frac{1}{2}} = 2\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Find the general equation of the tangent line\nUsing the point-slope form of a line, \\(y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)\\), where \\((x_1, y_1)\\) is the center of \\(C_1\\):\n\\[\ny - 2 = 2(x - 1)\n\\]\nSimplify to get the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\\[\ny - 2 = 2x - 2\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 2x\n\\]\n\n### Step 5: Verify the tangent line is tangent to \\(C_1\\) at \\(P_1\\) and tangent to \\(C_2\\) at \\(P_2\\)\nFor a line to be tangent to a circle, the distance from the center of the circle to the line must equal the radius of the circle.\n\nThe distance \\(d\\) from a point \\((x_0, y_0)\\) to the line \\(Ax + By + C = 0\\) is:\n\\[\nd = \\frac{|Ax_0 + By_0 + C|}{\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}}\n\\]\n\nFor the tangent line 